Hall of Universal Brightness 
INTERPRETATION 1 — Three Buddhas of Past, Present, Future
  Past:    Dīpaṅkara (燃燈佛)
  Present: Śākyamuni (釋迦牟尼佛)
  Future:  Maitreya (彌勒佛)

INTERPRETATION 2 — Trikāya (Three Bodies)
  Dharmakāya:    Truth Body
  Saṃbhogakāya:  Bliss/Enjoyment Body
  Nirmāṇakāya:   Manifestation Body

INTERPRETATION 3 — Three Directional Buddhas (橫三世佛)
  Centre:  Śākyamuni
  Left of Śākyamuni (viewer's right): Amitābha (Western Pure Land)
  Right of Śākyamuni (viewer's left): Bhaiṣajyaguru (Eastern Pure Land)
All three interpretations are simultaneously affirmed by the institution. This is itself the doctrinal claim the hall is making.
3. The doctrinal teaching. The KMSPKS explanation makes the underlying theological point explicit: "These interpretations remind us that all forms, including images of the Buddha, are inherently empty of any fixed essence, self-nature or substantiality." The Chinese version is more precise: 緣起性空 (yuánqǐ xìngkōng) — dependent origination and emptiness of self-nature. This is Madhyamaka śūnyatā teaching, materially encoded. The multiplicity of interpretations is not a hermeneutic embarrassment to be resolved; it is the doctrine itself. Because all forms are empty of fixed essence and arise dependently, no single interpretation can be definitive. The same three sculptural forms simultaneously are the three temporal Buddhas, the three Bodies of Buddhahood, and the three directional Pure Land Buddhas — and this very simultaneity demonstrates that no form has fixed essence.
The deliberate sculptural near-identity of the three figures, which seemed analytically puzzling in the previous reading, is now revealed as doctrinally necessary. If the three figures were iconographically differentiated by mudrā, attribute, or scale, they could only support one interpretation. Because they are formally near-identical, all three readings remain simultaneously available — and this availability is itself the teaching.

ICON descriptions
ICON₁ — Central Buddha
  Identification: Śākyamuni (under all three interpretations)
  POSITION:    central, axial
  SCALE:       equal (relative to flanks)
  ORIENTATION: front-facing
  FUNCTION:    soteriological, devotional, cosmological
  REGISTER:    Theravada-Burmese

ICON₂ — Left Buddha (viewer's left = Śākyamuni's right)
  Identification: Bhaiṣajyaguru / Eastern Pure Land Buddha
                  (under spatial-directional interpretation)
                  OR Past Buddha (Dīpaṅkara)
                  OR Dharmakāya / Saṃbhogakāya
  Same POSITION, SCALE, ORIENTATION, FUNCTION, REGISTER as ICON₁

ICON₃ — Right Buddha (viewer's right = Śākyamuni's left)
  Identification: Amitābha / Western Pure Land Buddha
                  (under spatial-directional interpretation)
                  OR Future Buddha (Maitreya)
                  OR remaining Trikāya body
  Same POSITION, SCALE, ORIENTATION, FUNCTION, REGISTER as ICON₁
The deliberate non-fixing of identity in ICON₂ and ICON₃ is faithful to the institutional theology. Each lateral figure is simultaneously three different Buddhas, and RFIG should document this multiplicity rather than collapse it into a single identification.

